{:schema 1
 :date "2026-08-14"
 :receipt/type :representation-experiment
 :follows "2026-08-14-the-gap-is-a-constant-factor.edn"

 :question
 "That receipt said the remaining 9x is in the representation and that the next
  experiment worth running is a representation experiment. This is it: what does
  a row cost if nothing but the covering indexes is kept?"

 :method
 "The same LDBC IC02 three-clause join, the same db, the same five start persons.
  Arm A is `dl/q`. Arm B walks `:pos {p {o #{s}}}` and `:spo {s {p #{o}}}`
  directly with nested loops into a transient vector -- no bindings, no
  {:s :p :o} per candidate, no set. Answers asserted equal on all five persons
  before either was timed. 10 warmup rounds, 60 timed samples, median."

 :result
 {:rows-per-query 910
  :a-engine-ms 18.640 :a-min-ms 17.088 :a-ns-per-row 20483
  :b-direct-walk-ms 0.465 :b-min-ms 0.368 :b-ns-per-row 511
  :b-faster-by "40.0x"
  :host-load 3.89}

 :the-reading
 "Neo4j is 9.7x faster than arm A on this query. Arm B is 40x faster than arm A,
  which puts it about 4x FASTER than Neo4j, over the same data in the same JVM.

  So the 9x is not a floor imposed by the JVM, by immutability, or by the index
  layout. The covering indexes already hold the data in a shape that answers this
  query faster than Neo4j does. What costs 40x is everything the engine puts
  between those indexes and the answer: a persistent-map binding extended per
  variable per row, an {:s :p :o} map per candidate, and a set at every step."

 :what-arm-b-is-not
 ["Not a proposal. It is a specialised walk for exactly this clause shape, with
   the join order hardcoded. It has no variables, no arbitrary clause count, no
   negation, rules, aggregates, `or`, or filters."
  "It applies no `visible?`. Authorization is a real per-row cost the engine must
   pay and arm B does not. `visible?` was `(constantly true)` in both arms, so
   this does not affect the comparison, but it does affect what a redesign could
   claim."
  "It returns a vector, not a set. The equality assertion compared `(set ...)` of
   it against the engine's set, so duplicates -- if any existed -- would have been
   hidden by that comparison. Deduplication is a cost arm B skipped."
  "One query shape, one dataset, one JVM. A bound, not a benchmark."]

 :what-it-establishes
 "A ceiling. Any redesign of the binding and intermediate representation is
  working in a range where 40x exists to be recovered, and where the target
  (Neo4j) sits well inside it. Before this, it was not known whether the 9x was
  reachable at all from the current data layout; it is, and by a margin.

  It also bounds the OTHER direction: whatever a general engine must keep --
  visibility, dedup, arbitrary clause shapes, rules -- has to fit inside that
  40x, and this experiment says nothing about how much of it that generality
  actually needs."

 :not-proposing-a-change
 "Six receipts of profiling produced 2-6% items. This produced a 40x bound and
  still does not name a mechanism, because a specialised walk is not evidence
  that a general engine can be made to walk that way. The next honest step is to
  find which of the three costs -- binding maps, quad maps, per-step sets -- a
  GENERAL join can actually shed, and that is a design question rather than a
  measurement."}
